parts.text:
Now edit the constructor chain. The 9-param constructor should delegate to a new 11-param. Then modify the 9-param body to the new constructor body with scheduler + hub. Let's replace carefully.

Existing:
```java
    public PathlandSession(
            String sessionId,
            StateStore store,
            PathlandApp app,
            EnvironmentData env,
            boolean debugHtml,
            String base,
            String stateScope,
            PathlandTelemetry telemetry,
            String mount) {
        this.debugHtml = ...
...
        RenderResult result = emitter.mount(...);
        ...
        applyEnvironment(env);
    }
```

Change to:
```java
    public PathlandSession(
            String sessionId,
            StateStore store,
            PathlandApp app,
            EnvironmentData env,
            boolean debugHtml,
            String base,
            String stateScope,
            PathlandTelemetry telemetry,
            String mount) {
        this(sessionId, store, app, env, debugHtml, base, stateScope, telemetry, mount, Runnable::run, null);
    }

    public PathlandSession(
            String sessionId,
            StateStore store,
            PathlandApp app,
            EnvironmentData env,
            boolean debugHtml,
            String base,
            String stateScope,
            PathlandTelemetry telemetry,
            String mount,
            Executor scheduler,
            PathlandSignalHub hub) {
        this.debugHtml = ...
        ...
        this.scheduler = scheduler == null ? Runnable::run : scheduler;
        this.subscriptions = new SessionSubscriptions(hub, this::submit, this.telemetry, this.mount);
        ...
        // Mount under the session context so a view can call topic(...) as a field.
        SessionContext previous = SessionContext.push(subscriptions);
        try {
            RenderResult result = emitter.mount(...);
            this.inputDispatcher = new InputDispatcher(result, activePath);
            this.rootId = result.rootId();
            this.proxyViews = result.proxyViews();
            this.emitter.onStructureReconciled(this::reconcileProxies);
            applyEnvironment(env);
        } finally {
            SessionContext.restore(previous);
        }
    }
```

One issue: `this.scheduler` and `this.subscriptions` must be assigned before `this::submit` is used; fine. `this::submit` references fields not yet assigned? Method reference captures `this`; when invoked later, fields assigned. Fine.

Another issue: final fields `inputDispatcher`, `rootId`, `proxyViews` assigned inside try. If an exception happens, constructor exits; Java allows definite assignment inside try? Let's confirm: final fields must be definitely assigned on normal completion. Assignments inside a try block are considered definitely assigned after the try if the try completes normally. If the try can throw, the constructor doesn't complete. This is allowed. Good.

`applyEnvironment` currently sets `activePath` and will be called within the context. But we will also wrap `applyEnvironment` for external calls; in the constructor, it's inside the context already, so calling the public method with its own context push would nest; that's fine (push/restore). But to avoid double wrapping, leave the public method wrapped; nested context is fine.

Let's apply the edit.

